00:16:22.986 --> 00:16:26.187
back in the Henan province in China at the museum over there,

00:16:26.727 --> 00:16:29.869
and was finally able to be studied and identified.

00:16:30.249 --> 00:16:34.351
And it has now been officially identified as a oviraptorosaur.

00:16:34.951 --> 00:16:37.852
So the oviraptorosaurs were these goofy looking dinosaurs,

00:16:38.052 --> 00:16:42.835
two-legged, you know, swarm-like necks, short faces with beaks.

00:16:43.215 --> 00:16:45.576
Flamingo beak almost with the weird downturn.

00:16:45.804 --> 00:16:48.405
Yeah, and a lot of them had weird crests on their heads.

00:16:49.085 --> 00:16:52.967
Darla Zelenitski, one of the authors of this new paper, compared them to cassowaries.

00:16:53.027 --> 00:16:56.688
Yeah, that's what I was about to say, they're like a whole bunch of birds smushed together.

00:16:57.309 --> 00:16:58.829
Yeah, they were pretty cool.

00:16:59.229 --> 00:17:03.031
But what's interesting is that based on the size of the eggs,

00:17:03.431 --> 00:17:10.174
they think it probably came from a giant species of oviraptorosaur, similar to gigantoraptor.

00:17:10.446 --> 00:17:16.949
Yeah, gigantoraptor is like a three-ton, eight-meter-long...

00:17:17.209 --> 00:17:23.972
This is a giant weird cassowary animal the size of Allosaurus from China.

00:17:24.213 --> 00:17:26.414
So baby lung is probably a baby.

00:17:26.514 --> 00:17:28.454
Oh, and I should point out that when I say baby,

00:17:28.835 --> 00:17:34.417
the evidence indicates that this skeleton was probably forced out of the egg,

00:17:35.125 --> 00:17:36.946
that it never actually hatched properly.

00:17:36.986 --> 00:17:38.246
So this is actually a fetus.

00:17:38.526 --> 00:17:43.448
Yeah, this is a fetal skeleton, ready to hatch, but it probably didn't actually hatch.

00:17:44.029 --> 00:17:47.430
So not only is it probably a giant new species,

00:17:47.830 --> 00:17:51.132
which is funny to me because it means someday if we hopefully find the adults,

00:17:51.212 --> 00:17:54.533
we will have a three-ton animal whose name is baby dragon.

00:17:57.285 --> 00:18:01.947
But the other thing that's cool is it identifies what type of dinosaur is laying these eggs.

00:18:03.027 --> 00:18:07.389
And these eggs are found, like I said, in North America, all across Asia,

00:18:07.950 --> 00:18:11.391
even though we haven't found giant oviraptorosaurs in all those places.

00:18:12.332 --> 00:18:18.014
So it tells us that there were probably these giant oviraptorosaurs living in

00:18:18.074 --> 00:18:21.195
North America as well that we just haven't found fossils of yet.

00:18:21.665 --> 00:18:25.927
The other really cool implication is that the structure of the nest

00:18:26.627 --> 00:18:30.109
is similar to some other oviraptor source, similar to some living birds,

00:18:31.289 --> 00:18:37.832
where you have these rings around an empty center, which in modern birds

00:18:38.513 --> 00:18:46.476
and in the extinct oviraptorosaurus city potty from Asia is where they sit to brood,

00:18:46.587 --> 00:18:49.988
to cover their nests during incubation.

00:18:50.348 --> 00:18:52.969
So this article, which was published in Nature,

00:18:53.849 --> 00:19:02.333
came out with an artistic reconstruction of a giant oviraptorosaur squatting down on its nest

00:19:02.373 --> 00:19:05.534
to cover up its eggs, which is awesome.

00:19:05.934 --> 00:19:08.375
It's really cool too because this isn't the first time

00:19:08.983 --> 00:19:13.225
really interesting discoveries have been made via nests when it comes to oviraptors.

00:19:13.945 --> 00:19:19.527
The name oviraptor means egg thief, because when the dinosaurs originally described,

00:19:19.688 --> 00:19:24.830
it was found seemingly burglarizing a dinosaur nest.

00:19:25.190 --> 00:19:25.810
Caught red-handed.

00:19:25.970 --> 00:19:27.751
Yeah, and it was a raptor.

00:19:27.771 --> 00:19:30.612
They assumed that it was guilty.

00:19:31.252 --> 00:19:33.213
That's theropod discrimination, that's what that is.

00:19:35.225 --> 00:19:43.569
They finally CT scanned the eggs and saw that actually what was inside the eggs

00:19:43.909 --> 00:19:49.372
were little oviraptors, and that this was not a thief, but a protective mother.

00:19:50.092 --> 00:19:50.492
Or father.

00:19:50.512 --> 00:19:55.435
You know, a protective parent brooding and guarding its nest.

00:19:56.644 --> 00:20:01.407
Potentially for something like a sandstorm or whatever it was that buried them all in the act,

00:20:01.967 --> 00:20:04.488
because it had its arms down, covering.

00:20:05.569 --> 00:20:09.811
And so these are animals that we keep getting to learn more about through eggs,

00:20:10.671 --> 00:20:13.993
which is just a neat parallel, I think.

00:20:14.453 --> 00:20:16.594
Yeah, it's a fun group.

00:20:16.794 --> 00:20:17.275
They're cool.

00:20:17.315 --> 00:20:18.015
They're cool animals.

00:20:18.055 --> 00:20:18.515
Weird looking.

00:20:18.855 --> 00:20:19.396
Very weird.

00:20:19.596 --> 00:20:20.536
Yeah, but cool.

