00:47:45.224 --> 00:47:50.007
we start seeing solid fossilizable parts calcified.

00:47:50.307 --> 00:47:51.247
That's a really good point.

00:47:51.527 --> 00:47:58.110
All those Ediacaran fossils, again, that's another episode, those are all impressions

00:47:58.470 --> 00:48:03.832
and soft tissue fossilization in super exceptional fossil sites.

00:48:03.912 --> 00:48:04.433
Exactly.

00:48:04.473 --> 00:48:09.335
So the Cambrian explosion is kind of this revolution of calcified body parts.

00:48:10.044 --> 00:48:18.508
Lots of arthropod-like and other shelled animals that started leaving behind these easily or

00:48:18.548 --> 00:48:20.188
more readily fossilized parts.

00:48:20.228 --> 00:48:27.952
This is when trilobites start showing up and dominated for 300 million years, just about.

00:48:28.012 --> 00:48:29.613
So that, once again, another episode.

00:48:29.653 --> 00:48:34.255
But trilobites show up here and everything else starts showing up.

00:48:34.604 --> 00:48:36.565
And we're not sure where they came from.

00:48:37.865 --> 00:48:43.768
We don't see any trilobite-like animals before the Cambrian.

00:48:43.908 --> 00:48:47.790
They show up and are already trilobites.

00:48:47.870 --> 00:48:52.332
It's sort of like when we were talking about the flight episodes is they showed up and

00:48:52.352 --> 00:48:57.114
we don't know how they got the way they were or remotely who they came from.

00:48:57.345 --> 00:49:03.707
As we said in that episode, the thing that tends to happen is when a group gets started,

00:49:04.287 --> 00:49:07.969
it's one population and then they diversify from there.

00:49:07.989 --> 00:49:13.391
So the earliest days, the earliest generations of any group, they're minor and they're not

00:49:13.671 --> 00:49:15.312
widespread and they're not really important.

00:49:15.772 --> 00:49:20.674
And so they're very unlikely to fossilize and those are the times where you start, where

00:49:20.704 --> 00:49:25.907
all that important evolution is happening, developing this new diversity.

00:49:26.807 --> 00:49:33.590
And there are some cases, many cases where we managed to get snippets of that.

00:49:34.411 --> 00:49:40.714
And there are bits along the progress of the Cambrian explosion where we see these stages

00:49:40.966 --> 00:49:46.008
of these ecosystems developing, but it's quick, you know, geologically.

00:49:46.568 --> 00:49:52.130
So again, 10, 20 million years is quick and it's not a lot of time for the fossilization

00:49:52.150 --> 00:49:52.731
of these steps.

00:49:53.231 --> 00:50:00.014
And that brings us well into the next section, which is the Cambrian explosion shaped a lot

00:50:00.024 --> 00:50:08.008
of the ways we now view fossil record evolution and the ecosystems that we see because this

00:50:08.588 --> 00:50:13.610
undid the system that had been and set into place many of the ones we recognize or the

00:50:14.110 --> 00:50:15.591
baseline that we recognize today.

00:50:15.611 --> 00:50:21.154
- Yeah, we should mention for the listeners that when we say modern groups of animals,

00:50:21.405 --> 00:50:27.848
we're meaning the first arthropods, the first worms, the earliest signs of chordates, which

00:50:27.908 --> 00:50:34.151
is our own group, the ancestors of vertebrates, you know, mollusks and brachiopods and pretty

00:50:34.211 --> 00:50:40.194
much all the groups that make up ecosystems today, but more important than that, we also

00:50:40.226 --> 00:50:46.649
see the beginnings of niches being filled, the first evidences of burrowers that burrow

00:50:46.689 --> 00:50:51.691
under the sediment, the first evidence of predators, like actual predators, animals

00:50:51.731 --> 00:50:53.272
that are eating other animals.

00:50:53.292 --> 00:50:55.533
- Yeah, they're very actively hunting.

00:50:55.553 --> 00:51:03.956
- The structure of ecosystems was formulated during the first 10, 20 million years of

00:51:04.225 --> 00:51:10.448
the Cambrian, even habitat structure, like the burrowing changed the structure of the

00:51:10.508 --> 00:51:10.968
sediment.

00:51:10.988 --> 00:51:11.588
- Exactly.

00:51:11.768 --> 00:51:14.829
They called it the Cambrian substrate revolution.

00:51:14.849 --> 00:51:21.332
- Yeah, which sets, that's the foundation, literally, on which ecosystems are formed

00:51:21.372 --> 00:51:21.732
today.

00:51:22.232 --> 00:51:25.914
It wiped out those old microbial mat dominated ecosystems.

00:51:26.503 --> 00:51:34.166
Now today, they only persist in some areas, and animals took over all these brand new niches,

00:51:34.927 --> 00:51:40.149
which is really cool, including a bunch of animals that don't necessarily fit any of

00:51:40.189 --> 00:51:41.210
the groups we see today.

00:51:41.230 --> 00:51:44.991
- Yep, and that's the thing, is there was lots of animals that we're not sure where

00:51:45.031 --> 00:51:47.512
they go, because they're weird.

00:51:47.532 --> 00:51:50.413
- Yeah, and they might not go anywhere.

00:51:50.443 --> 00:51:56.666
They might indeed be phyla that started out and didn't make it past the Cambrian.

00:51:56.686 --> 00:52:03.549
- And so the fossil record for the Cambrian is very vast, especially due to sites like

00:52:03.569 --> 00:52:06.410
the Burgess Shale, which many people have probably heard of.

00:52:06.430 --> 00:52:14.214
It's a very famous one, and this is a site where very ideal fossilization conditions

00:52:14.546 --> 00:52:20.188
have not only preserved many, many animals, but soft tissue of the animals have been preserved

00:52:20.248 --> 00:52:27.392
in outlines and in impressions, meaning that we have some soft body animals preserved there

00:52:27.432 --> 00:52:33.154
that you would not find with any of the calcified fossils, which has increased the amount we

00:52:33.214 --> 00:52:34.134
know even further.

00:52:34.545 --> 00:52:42.389
And there are other Burgess Shale type deposits around the world, and they even call the fossilization

00:52:42.749 --> 00:52:44.570
Burgess Shale type preservation.

00:52:44.750 --> 00:52:49.212
It's named after their site because of that detail that it can get.

00:52:49.232 --> 00:52:53.194
- Yeah, the Burgess Shale is in British Columbia in Canada.

00:52:53.495 --> 00:52:59.358
There's actually a handful of these really awesome early Cambrian and Adiacaran sites

00:52:59.405 --> 00:53:00.366
in Canada.

00:53:00.386 --> 00:53:02.227
- There's another famous one in China.

00:53:02.247 --> 00:53:04.188
I can't remember the pronunciation of it.

00:53:04.208 --> 00:53:05.048
- Chengjiang.

00:53:05.148 --> 00:53:11.031
I have a book that is essentially a field guide to the Chengjiang fauna, and it's pictures

00:53:11.191 --> 00:53:17.534
of all these earliest like trilobite type things, anomalocaris type things, which were

00:53:17.574 --> 00:53:22.196
the swimming shrimpy predator things with claw faces, and then stuff like walaxia and

00:53:22.225 --> 00:53:26.667
hallucigenia that we just have no idea what the heck that is.

00:53:26.687 --> 00:53:30.268
- Yes, and this was a time where animals we recognized were showing up, animals we didn't

00:53:30.308 --> 00:53:34.370
recognize were showing up, and they all seemed to come out of nowhere.

00:53:35.410 --> 00:53:42.153
And to quickly, because it's worth mentioning, the history of the impression among the scientific

00:53:42.173 --> 00:53:46.875
community for the Cambrian explosion is worth mentioning because it is interesting and important

00:53:47.206 --> 00:53:51.988
because it's super weird, and it was realized pretty early on that it was super weird.

00:53:53.429 --> 00:53:58.171
So first Cambrian animals ever found were trilobites, the most famous of the Cambrian

00:53:58.211 --> 00:53:59.211
animals by far.

00:53:59.651 --> 00:54:06.314
And this is like going back into the 1700s, 1698 I think is when they were being described.

00:54:06.865 --> 00:54:14.608
And these animals were heavily used for dating rock layers and actually were used to date

00:54:15.148 --> 00:54:18.890
the layers of rocks that would become the Cambrian or become known as the Cambrian.

00:54:20.170 --> 00:54:25.193
So they were used mainly for, when they were first discovered, their heaviest use was actually

00:54:25.253 --> 00:54:27.113
in IDing those layers.

00:54:27.804 --> 00:54:31.686
But then it was pretty quickly realized that something weird was going on.

00:54:32.146 --> 00:54:39.770
Charles Walcott was the man who discovered and studied the Burgess Shale and who first

00:54:40.210 --> 00:54:45.652
set out the trilobite-based biostratigraphy that IDed the Cambrian period.

00:54:46.213 --> 00:54:51.775
So he did a whole bunch, IDed, wrote down the information, and described many of the

00:54:51.835 --> 00:54:52.195
animals.

00:54:52.605 --> 00:55:00.789
And then after that, Herring Whittington came along and he and his colleagues studied his

00:55:00.849 --> 00:55:01.129
works.

00:55:01.890 --> 00:55:05.712
And they were the first ones that really kind of came to the conclusion that something weird

00:55:06.132 --> 00:55:06.692
happened here.

00:55:07.132 --> 00:55:10.294
Yeah, we went from no fossils to lots of fossils.

00:55:10.394 --> 00:55:14.737
So they were the first ones that really kind of pointed a finger saying there's a divide

00:55:14.804 --> 00:55:18.386
here where animals just kind of seem to show up.

00:55:18.526 --> 00:55:18.826
Yeah.

00:55:19.246 --> 00:55:22.747
And this was the time at which people were starting to really scratch their heads as

00:55:23.047 --> 00:55:24.988
are we looking at the origin of life?

00:55:25.028 --> 00:55:26.329
Are we looking at something weird?

00:55:27.589 --> 00:55:31.511
My favorite of those fingers being pointed at the issues is Darwin actually mentioned

00:55:31.551 --> 00:55:37.593
it in The Origin of Species toward the end as one of the greatest potential arguments

00:55:37.773 --> 00:55:39.374
against his theory.

00:55:40.346 --> 00:55:46.129
Because as he put it, if these animals, which are already complex and diverse, truly did

00:55:46.229 --> 00:55:51.331
just show up here, an animal like this should have an ancestor it came from.

00:55:51.772 --> 00:55:54.953
There should be a common ancestor, but there is no common ancestor.

00:55:54.973 --> 00:55:57.795
And if that's the case, that's a big hole.

00:55:58.095 --> 00:55:58.355
Yeah.

00:55:58.895 --> 00:56:02.677
Now his answer at the time was that our fossil record is incomplete.

00:56:02.977 --> 00:56:03.177
Right.

00:56:03.664 --> 00:56:05.705
And we'll find it eventually.

00:56:05.865 --> 00:56:06.265
Exactly.

00:56:06.285 --> 00:56:08.266
There must be these fossils.

00:56:08.306 --> 00:56:09.427
We just have not found it yet.

00:56:09.767 --> 00:56:12.328
He even posited a potential answer.

00:56:12.388 --> 00:56:19.532
He says, I do not have a good enough answer, but if I had to give one, it would be that

00:56:19.992 --> 00:56:26.576
the areas that we find these blank stratigraphy, a strata of rock beneath the Cambrian that

00:56:26.647 --> 00:56:32.289
have seemingly no fossils, were in areas where fossilization was just not able to happen.

00:56:32.529 --> 00:56:32.869
Yeah.

00:56:33.369 --> 00:56:37.851
And that we have these giant blocks of time where fossils just weren't being able to be

00:56:37.891 --> 00:56:38.291
created.

00:56:38.871 --> 00:56:39.151
Yeah.

00:56:39.292 --> 00:56:44.694
He had an explanation having to do with the large islands or expanses, and I'd have to

00:56:45.294 --> 00:56:46.474
double check exactly what he said.

00:56:46.514 --> 00:56:48.275
But yeah, I don't remember exactly.

00:56:48.495 --> 00:56:49.736
It was interesting.

00:56:49.903 --> 00:56:53.525
Even Darwin was recognizing something weird here, and it's an issue.

00:56:54.446 --> 00:56:58.688
It's funny because, like you said, he says, I don't know what's going on.

00:56:58.708 --> 00:57:03.891
There should be a progression that shows the origin and gradual evolution here.

00:57:04.471 --> 00:57:05.071
We don't see it.

00:57:05.091 --> 00:57:06.212
I'm sure it's out there.

00:57:06.312 --> 00:57:09.954
We just haven't found it yet because their record is bad.

00:57:10.134 --> 00:57:10.294
Yeah.

00:57:10.414 --> 00:57:15.017
And you can imagine people of the time being like, uh-huh.

00:57:15.224 --> 00:57:18.405
That is the least convincing argument.

00:57:18.425 --> 00:57:18.605
Yeah.

00:57:18.945 --> 00:57:19.826
Like, yeah, sure.

00:57:21.867 --> 00:57:22.607
I'm sure we'll find it.

00:57:23.748 --> 00:57:25.809
The cool thing, of course, is that, yeah, we have.

00:57:25.869 --> 00:57:26.569
Yes, we have.

00:57:27.230 --> 00:57:33.933
We absolutely have found, like I said, there's life beforehand, and we do have at least a

00:57:33.953 --> 00:57:37.335
little bit of the layers of progression throughout this explosion.

00:57:38.226 --> 00:57:39.667
So if he were here today--

00:57:39.687 --> 00:57:46.049
I used to wonder that-- this is a quick aside for everyone-- but one of the things that

00:57:46.149 --> 00:57:50.511
baffled Darwin the most was how traits were passed down because they had not discovered

00:57:50.531 --> 00:57:57.555
genetics yet, and he continued to try to rewrite his theory over and over to adjust for it.

00:57:58.035 --> 00:58:00.576
And I can only imagine how maddening that must have been.

00:58:00.865 --> 00:58:03.987
And if only we could show-- it's like, no, no, you were on the right course.

00:58:04.547 --> 00:58:07.008
We discovered these things inside our bodies.

00:58:07.548 --> 00:58:10.610
He was-- it's-- if you haven't read The Origin of Species, go read it.

00:58:10.890 --> 00:58:11.110
Yes.

00:58:11.250 --> 00:58:13.291
It's astonishing how close he was.

00:58:13.551 --> 00:58:14.912
It's really amazing.

00:58:15.352 --> 00:58:17.973
He was-- he had the idea of genetics.

00:58:18.113 --> 00:58:20.874
He was right there, hadn't been discovered yet.

00:58:21.695 --> 00:58:22.455
That's a whole other episode.

00:58:22.595 --> 00:58:22.775
Yeah.

00:58:23.082 --> 00:58:29.946
So last note on the history, I wanted to mention Preston Cloud, who was one of the-- he was

00:58:29.966 --> 00:58:32.027
kind of a jack-of-all-trades from the sounds of it.

00:58:32.108 --> 00:58:33.508
He had a giant sword.

00:58:33.648 --> 00:58:34.249
Yeah, right.

00:58:34.409 --> 00:58:35.089
And crazy hair.

00:58:35.249 --> 00:58:37.911
Yeah, it was really-- he did a lot.

00:58:37.931 --> 00:58:42.214
He was-- he-- geology, paleontology, monster-slaying.

00:58:42.734 --> 00:58:44.195
Yeah, saved the Earth from an asteroid.

00:58:44.295 --> 00:58:44.935
It was crazy.

00:58:45.126 --> 00:58:46.168
He could summon a big dragon.

00:58:46.188 --> 00:58:47.269
[LAUGHTER]

00:58:49.524 --> 00:58:50.764
I've never played these games.

00:58:50.784 --> 00:58:53.205
It's the first video game reference on our podcast.

00:58:53.225 --> 00:58:54.506
I have also never played those games.

00:58:54.526 --> 00:58:55.226
I've never played this.

00:58:55.727 --> 00:58:57.428
I know all of this from Advent Children.

00:58:58.848 --> 00:58:59.469
I'm sorry, continue.

00:59:00.349 --> 00:59:04.171
He was the one who really put the Cameron Explosion in the viewpoint that we kind of

00:59:04.191 --> 00:59:08.633
have it today, to almost the point of naming it the Cameron Explosion.

00:59:08.685 --> 00:59:14.327
He has one quote that I'll have to find and put in the blog post that is so close to calling

00:59:14.387 --> 00:59:20.870
it Explosion, but not quite so-- but that's really the man that framed it all in the way

00:59:20.910 --> 00:59:22.251
that it's typically framed today.

00:59:22.691 --> 00:59:29.054
But since all of that, we have found a lot of the information and now don't have the

00:59:29.145 --> 00:59:34.487
answers, but have more potential answers and at least know better which questions to ask.

00:59:34.927 --> 00:59:40.509
Yeah, there's a-- it's-- like I said, one of those situations where we have too many

00:59:40.549 --> 00:59:41.090
good options.

00:59:41.370 --> 00:59:41.670
Yeah.

00:59:42.490 --> 00:59:45.211
We haven't found the evidence yet that tells us which one is the right one.

00:59:45.532 --> 00:59:47.833
One paper I found really makes that point.

00:59:47.893 --> 00:59:53.435
It was written by Charles R. Marshall, and his whole thing was the saying that we have

00:59:53.464 --> 00:59:57.006
all these different options typically coming from different backgrounds of science.

00:59:57.926 --> 01:00:02.889
And it very well could be a combination of them or a interaction of them and trying to

01:00:02.929 --> 01:00:08.171
figure out-- and I believe he was even doing it mathematically, trying to figure out which

01:00:08.211 --> 01:00:11.832
were-- it started getting into stuff that I don't know how to dabble with.

01:00:12.986 --> 01:00:20.310
I'm basically trying to say what caused this sudden diversification, this massive event.

01:00:20.670 --> 01:00:27.133
And there are typically three main factors that people look to as causes, many causes

01:00:27.173 --> 01:00:32.436
within each factor or potential causes, but they're either looking at the abiotic environment,

01:00:32.456 --> 01:00:35.317
meaning the actual physical environment.

01:00:36.125 --> 01:00:38.866
Right, something changed in the habitat.

01:00:39.086 --> 01:00:39.586
Exactly.

01:00:40.467 --> 01:00:46.249
Genetic or developmental capacity, meaning that something about the DNA of life was able

01:00:46.269 --> 01:00:52.712
to give rise to this explosion, something that changed in the base genetics, in evolution

01:00:52.752 --> 01:00:53.693
itself.

01:00:54.245 --> 01:01:00.988
Or the ecological, something changed in the interactions of the animals and the life.

01:01:01.649 --> 01:01:08.152
And each of them have potential answers with potential problems as to those answers.

01:01:08.788 --> 01:01:15.292
And I'll go through these semi-quick because, once again, each of these could vary-- we

01:01:15.312 --> 01:01:18.893
could go through each potential one in an episode itself because I'm sure there are

01:01:18.908 --> 01:01:22.850
a dozen papers on each one with how studied this is.

01:01:23.350 --> 01:01:30.293
The environmental effects typically fall either in an increase of oxygen or snowball earth,

01:01:30.413 --> 01:01:35.476
which I'll explain in a second, or something-- the last two are a bit weird-- the carbon

01:01:35.567 --> 01:01:40.229
isotope mass extinction or true polar wander are the four that I found.

01:01:41.469 --> 01:01:43.610
More oxygen is pretty straightforward.

01:01:43.930 --> 01:01:46.852
More oxygen, more metabolism, more life.

01:01:47.632 --> 01:01:49.013
Bigger bodies, more diversity.

01:01:49.393 --> 01:01:52.394
It's a common thing that we have seen in the fossil record before.

01:01:53.014 --> 01:01:56.476
When we had large arthropods on land, there was higher oxygen.

01:01:57.084 --> 01:02:01.827
They could breathe more easily since they don't have powered breathing, so they could

01:02:01.867 --> 01:02:03.227
survive as being bigger.

01:02:03.567 --> 01:02:08.029
And there is some evidence for changes in oxygen level around this time leading into

01:02:08.049 --> 01:02:08.490
the Cambrian.

01:02:08.830 --> 01:02:15.153
Now, the biggest issue that many people point out is we don't know what the oxygen requirements

01:02:15.233 --> 01:02:20.915
for those first animals would have been, nor do we have precise values for what it was

01:02:21.067 --> 01:02:25.569
before the Cambrian to know if those levels were already at that.

01:02:25.709 --> 01:02:29.210
So not 100%, but it points to it.

01:02:29.310 --> 01:02:34.373
The one big thing that it points to, going back to the Ediacarans, is that some of them

01:02:34.413 --> 01:02:35.133
were already big.

01:02:35.953 --> 01:02:38.074
So big life was already possible.

01:02:38.354 --> 01:02:39.115
Yeah, it was happening.

01:02:39.255 --> 01:02:40.395
It started.

01:02:40.463 --> 01:02:46.486
Snowball Earth is another big one, which this is the idea that Earth, for a time, was completely

01:02:47.107 --> 01:02:48.828
covered by glaciers, basically.

01:02:49.508 --> 01:02:51.649
Yeah, glaciation to the tropics.

01:02:52.170 --> 01:02:58.053
And this would have-- when you get a-- the Snowball Earth idea has kind of a double effect

01:02:58.194 --> 01:03:01.516
of because it's becoming more white, it reflects more sunlight.

01:03:01.985 --> 01:03:05.506
And as it reflects more sunlight, it gets colder, and more things freeze, becomes more

01:03:05.546 --> 01:03:06.687
white, reflects more sunlight.

01:03:06.847 --> 01:03:08.728
It's like the opposite of the greenhouse effect.

01:03:09.408 --> 01:03:12.209
Yeah, it's the albedo effect.

01:03:12.609 --> 01:03:19.792
The idea here is that at the end of Snowball Earth, it would have opened up massive amounts

01:03:19.852 --> 01:03:23.874
of new habitat for that diversification.

01:03:24.486 --> 01:03:31.249
Issue with this answer being that evidence shows the last major global glaciation was

01:03:31.269 --> 01:03:37.131
635 million years ago, which would put it 90 million years before the Cambrian.

01:03:37.372 --> 01:03:41.733
Yeah, so it took-- if that's the reason, it took a little bit-- there's lag time before

01:03:42.014 --> 01:03:43.054
it took off.

01:03:43.324 --> 01:03:50.447
Now, there is-- people do usually connect the Ediacaran biota with a glaciation event.

01:03:50.567 --> 01:03:54.889
The Gaskiers-- I'm assuming it's German.

01:03:55.330 --> 01:04:00.212
But the Gaskiers glaciation, the end of it coincides pretty well with the beginnings

01:04:00.292 --> 01:04:02.813
of the Ediacaran biota.

01:04:02.913 --> 01:04:07.195
So all this life cooped up by the glaciation, just waiting to diversify when it left.

01:04:07.425 --> 01:04:09.886
And so that's the idea, but on a global scale.

01:04:10.526 --> 01:04:15.029
Once again, it's not-- it hasn't been a perfect answer, but it's a potential one.

01:04:15.549 --> 01:04:20.952
The last two are very interesting. The carbon isotope mass extinction is an interesting

01:04:21.012 --> 01:04:28.316
one, because one thing we do see in the Cambrian is a large negative carbon isotope anomaly.

01:04:28.627 --> 01:04:34.029
So there's suddenly this large change in the amount of negative carbon isotopes in the

01:04:34.089 --> 01:04:34.509
environment.

