When the dinosaur, the reign of the dinosaurs ended, mammals diversified really quick.

01:20:53.410 --> 01:20:57.013
This is that when that happened with animals.

01:20:57.393 --> 01:20:57.953
With everything.

01:20:58.614 --> 01:21:00.875
All of them.

01:21:00.883 --> 01:21:06.506
So there are two really cool things to say here at the end before we end the episode.

01:21:06.526 --> 01:21:07.586
So just little side notes.

01:21:08.487 --> 01:21:10.668
The why it's important and why it's weird.

01:21:10.728 --> 01:21:12.649
We've talked a good bit about why it's weird.

01:21:12.969 --> 01:21:19.032
The why it's important is more so than just the fact that it gave rise to everything we

01:21:19.072 --> 01:21:25.395
recognize now, but it also reshaped the way we look at evolution and the fossil record.

01:21:25.665 --> 01:21:33.028
And particularly in one case, this is what gave rise to or sparked the idea of punctuated

01:21:33.068 --> 01:21:33.688
equilibrium.

01:21:34.689 --> 01:21:36.249
Ah, also Stephen Jay Gould.

01:21:36.609 --> 01:21:42.871
This event, so punctuated equilibrium is an idea in evolution that it does not always

01:21:42.911 --> 01:21:43.772
happen slowly.

01:21:44.112 --> 01:21:50.274
So the Darwinian school of thought when he first put his ideas to paper was that evolution

01:21:50.304 --> 01:21:57.807
is a very slow, gradual, fairly regular process that, you know, mutations, he didn't know

01:21:57.847 --> 01:22:04.910
at the time, but mutations build up at the normal rate and things progress slowly and

01:22:04.970 --> 01:22:08.512
grudgingly building up enough changes to become something different.

01:22:08.806 --> 01:22:16.450
Punctuated equilibrium is saying, okay, yes, but then there are these short moments of

01:22:17.430 --> 01:22:25.254
surprisingly explosively fast periods of evolution where things line up right and you have a

01:22:25.474 --> 01:22:31.997
sudden diversity of birds or things open up and you have a whole bunch of terrestrial

01:22:32.047 --> 01:22:36.648
animals that suddenly diversify and that there are these moments where evolution speeds up

01:22:36.708 --> 01:22:37.129
suddenly.

01:22:37.369 --> 01:22:43.731
Yeah, and you can see that even not with just with big groups of animals, but with traits.

01:22:43.911 --> 01:22:44.532
Yes, right.

01:22:44.672 --> 01:22:50.634
If you're a species and you have a certain trait that works, right, if you're an elephant

01:22:50.694 --> 01:22:55.536
and the world is nice and warm, everything's fine when the world gets cold, right?

01:22:55.565 --> 01:23:00.567
If the climate drops quickly or rises quickly, you're going to be under pretty intense

01:23:00.627 --> 01:23:04.008
selection to adapt a new trait.

01:23:04.648 --> 01:23:11.031
So the trait of being, you know, woolier, if you're not wooly and it gets, you know,

01:23:11.091 --> 01:23:17.133
and the environment changes quickly, that pretty quickly is going to weed out everybody

01:23:17.173 --> 01:23:18.214
without the right trait.

01:23:18.334 --> 01:23:18.914
Exactly.

01:23:19.542 --> 01:23:23.705
And so that's going to be a rapid period of evolutionary change.

01:23:23.725 --> 01:23:32.770
Exactly, and so this is a view on evolution that was originally sparked because of the

01:23:32.790 --> 01:23:35.511
Cambrian explosion or at least had its origins.

01:23:35.871 --> 01:23:39.513
I don't know whether the term was used back then, but it had its origins there of saying,

01:23:39.553 --> 01:23:43.195
hey, maybe this was just really, really fast evolution.

01:23:43.785 --> 01:23:46.286
Things didn't just appear here, but they just evolved so quickly.

01:23:46.346 --> 01:23:49.507
We don't have the full record of it.

01:23:49.547 --> 01:23:50.968
It looks like they popped in.

01:23:51.488 --> 01:23:51.728
Yeah.

01:23:51.968 --> 01:23:54.470
It was faster for us to see in geological time.

01:23:54.770 --> 01:24:00.973
Yeah, and these days we have pretty, you know, there's still discussion over when the rates

01:24:01.013 --> 01:24:05.915
of evolution, but there's pretty good evidence that, yeah, no, evolution is not consistent

01:24:05.975 --> 01:24:06.535
in its need.

01:24:07.164 --> 01:24:12.487
You have really slow, you know, periods where there's not a whole lot of change and periods

01:24:12.507 --> 01:24:15.028
where you get some really intense adaptation.

01:24:15.168 --> 01:24:15.708
Exactly.

01:24:15.928 --> 01:24:20.590
Now, the last thing I want to end the episode on before we say our goodbyes and everything

01:24:20.951 --> 01:24:27.354
is the one, one of the big questions about the Cambrian explosion that must be asked,

01:24:27.414 --> 01:24:31.436
especially with, you know, certain bits of evidence is, was there really an explosion?

01:24:31.985 --> 01:24:38.408
Was this actually an explosion or is it really, is it just normal and this is just what we

01:24:38.528 --> 01:24:38.868
have?

01:24:39.849 --> 01:24:44.070
The big part of that being what Darwin suggested is preservation biased.

01:24:44.911 --> 01:24:45.131
Yes.

01:24:45.771 --> 01:24:48.232
We have an incomplete fossil record.

01:24:48.252 --> 01:24:50.453
We've known this since we've ever started finding fossils.

01:24:50.973 --> 01:24:55.235
It is the main truth of fossil hunting and study.

01:24:55.515 --> 01:24:55.755
Yeah.

01:24:56.143 --> 01:25:00.946
Not everything fossilizes, not even most things fossilize, most things don't.

01:25:01.866 --> 01:25:05.668
And even those things that do, much of it doesn't even survive to be found.

01:25:07.449 --> 01:25:13.591
So it is very possible that the ancestors to all these animals had been developing and

01:25:13.632 --> 01:25:19.154
evolving for a very long, gradual time and we just don't have those fossils.

01:25:19.646 --> 01:25:24.287
And that's supported by the fact that a lot of the animals that show up, we don't just

01:25:24.407 --> 01:25:29.970
find, you know, the first animals and it's like, Oh look, this is starting to show those

01:25:30.010 --> 01:25:30.310
traits.

01:25:30.490 --> 01:25:37.012
Like when we find the first trilobites, they are trilobites, fully formed, developed, you

01:25:37.032 --> 01:25:40.854
know, all of the complexity that we think of a trilobite and diverse.

01:25:41.486 --> 01:25:46.628
They were a very diverse group and when looking at the molecular clocks, which is a term we

01:25:46.708 --> 01:25:54.532
use when we try to average the rates of mutation buildup, which is an exact practice because

01:25:54.592 --> 01:26:00.194
it's once again debated as to how quickly those actually build up and they will vary

01:26:00.995 --> 01:26:03.136
under the punctuated equilibrium idea.

01:26:03.225 --> 01:26:08.307
But looking at the potential molecular clocks, place them well before the beginning for the

01:26:08.467 --> 01:26:10.207
origins of trilobites of the Cambrian.

01:26:10.448 --> 01:26:10.688
Right.

01:26:10.748 --> 01:26:15.970
So the genetic evidence gives us reason to think that there would have been, the origin

01:26:16.030 --> 01:26:17.991
actually lies a little bit earlier than this.

01:26:18.051 --> 01:26:23.153
That there should have been pre-Cambrian trilobites all over the place that we're just not finding

01:26:23.213 --> 01:26:23.893
for some reason.

01:26:24.694 --> 01:26:26.174
Which of course makes perfect sense.

01:26:26.243 --> 01:26:31.225
Anytime you have a diversity of organisms, yeah, you're missing the part where they diversify.

01:26:31.685 --> 01:26:38.028
And so the idea here is exactly what Darwin first suggested, that we, especially because

01:26:38.228 --> 01:26:42.310
the animals we find in the Cambrian are, many of them, complex already.

01:26:43.251 --> 01:26:49.494
We're just missing the long, long period of time where they were becoming complex.

01:26:50.426 --> 01:26:51.787
And we just don't have those fossils.

01:26:52.367 --> 01:26:59.710
So there may not really be an explosion, we just may be coming in mid-season and going,

